<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy IV Philopator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 244–<a href="/years/204-bce">204 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptolemy IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy IV Philopator was king of Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 221 to 204 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a> he defeated the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a> in 217 BCE, with the help of native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> trained as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> soldiers. His reign, dominated by courtiers such as Sosibius, saw the start of native Egyptian revolts that split the kingdom for two decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator.jpg" alt="Ptolemy IV Philopator" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octadrachm_Ptolemy_IV_BM_CMBMC33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy IV was the second child and eldest son of Ptolemy III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-ii">Berenice II</a>. He had an older sister, Arsinoe III, and three younger brothers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, Alexander and Magas, all born in the 240s BCE. Under Ptolemy III the kingdom had reached its height, though that reign had also seen the first native Egyptian revolt, in 245 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The new king was about twenty and under the strong influence of the aristocrats Sosibius and Agathocles, brother of the king's mistress Agathoclea. Sosibius engineered a purge of the royal family. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> was probably murdered, and his brother Magas, popular with the army, was scalded to death in his bath. Ptolemy married Arsinoe III by 220 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, who had taken the Seleucid throne in 222 BCE, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> and in 219 BCE captured Seleucia Pieria. In early 218 BCE he destroyed Ptolemaic forces at Berytus. On 22 June 217 BCE, Ptolemy and Arsinoe led the Egyptian army against him at Raphia, one of the largest battles of the Hellenistic Age, with over 150,000 soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, generally hostile to Ptolemy, presents the king's sudden appearance on the front line as the turning point. Antiochus retreated, and the peace left Ptolemy with the territories he had held at the start, apparently except Seleucia Pieria. A priestly synod at Memphis issued the Raphia decree.</p><p>Ptolemy proclaimed himself a god, the Theos Philopator, and systematised the dynastic cult. He promoted the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and was called the Neos Dionysos. He supported Egyptian temples, notably the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>, and is said to have built a huge galley, the tessarakonteres. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> he helped broker the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-naupactus">Peace of Naupactus</a> in 217 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war a great revolt broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In October or November 205 BCE the leader of the southern rebels took Thebes and was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horwennefer">Horwennefer</a>, and he kept his independence for nearly twenty years, until captured in August 186 BCE. Polybius held that the revolts resulted from arming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> for the war.</p><p>Ptolemy died in July or August 204 BCE in unclear circumstances, and Arsinoe III died at about the same time. His six-year-old son Ptolemy V, co-regent since 210 BCE, was proclaimed king with Sosibius and Agathocles as regents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, the main source, portrays Ptolemy as a bad king absorbed in luxury, but he was not his contemporary, and luxury was often counted a virtue by those who lived then.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy IV Philopator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For the campaign that ended in the Battle of Raphia in 217 BCE, <b>Ptolemy IV</b> recruited and trained some 20,000 native Egyptians in the Macedonian phalanx, and their part in the victory over Antiochus III marked a turning point.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy V Epiphanes became king at the age of five after his parents were murdered in a conspiracy that, according to contemporary sources, involved <b>Ptolemy IV</b>'s mistress Agathoclea.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ptolemy IV</b> royal favourites controlled the government, and in 205 BCE a priest, Horwennefer, proclaimed himself pharaoh in Upper Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ptolemy IV Philopator</b> of Egypt defeated the Seleucid king Antiochus III the Great near present-day Rafah in the Fourth Syrian War, with native Egyptian troops taking a large part.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
